    'The league experiment uses a ZPD band of 3.5-5.5 workspace units and trains ten robot generations without providing an explicit opponent identity. The absence of opponent identity makes the task more representative of rehabilitation, where the controller must infer patient capability from motion history rather than from a predefined label. Each robot generation is evaluated against the learned hand pool and a scripted pursuit controller. Time-in-zone (TIS), defined as the fraction of interaction time spent inside the ZPD band, is used as the main robustness metric, with both mean and worst-case values reported to capture average performance and lower-tail robustness.',

